## DBsubject(Mechanics)
## DBchapter(Oscillatory Motion and Waves)
## DBsection(Superposition and Interference)
## Date(December 2017)
## Institution(Brock University)
## Author(Brandon Lostracco)
## Edited (Connor Wilson, May 1, 2018)
## TitleText1('College Physics')
## AuthorText1('Urone et. al')
## EditionText1('2017')
## Section1('16.10')
## Problem1('5')
## KEYWORDS(frequency, velocity, wave, wavelength)

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context('Numeric');

$time     = random(2, 4, 0.1);
$distance = random(2, 8, 0.1);
$A1       = (2) * ($distance) / ($time);
$A2       = ($A1) / (($distance) / (1.5));

BEGIN_PGML
A wave traveling on a Slinky® that is stretched to [`[$distance]`] [`\textrm{m}`] takes [`[$time]`] [`\textrm{s}`] to travel the length of the Slinky and back again.

a) What is the speed of the wave? [_]{$A1} J

b) Using the same Slinky stretched to the same length, a standing wave is created which consists of three antinodes and four nodes. At what frequency must the Slinky be oscillating?

    [_]{$A2} Hz

END_PGML
BEGIN_PGML_HINT
a)  Recall speed is defined as the change in distance divided by the change in time. Be careful - the time given is that taken for the wave to travel _twice_ the length of the Slinky.

b) Recall the formula relating the velocity of a wave to its frequency and wavelength. What is the wavelength of the wave, given that a full wavelength (_i.e._ one cycle of a sine wave) consists of three nodes and two antinodes? It may help you to draw a diagram.
END_PGML_HINT

ENDDOCUMENT();
